      "writtenOn": "2024-08-30T16:35:52Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "\u003e Sounds good, but then it doesn\u0027t match the logic mentioned in go/bcic-care. \n\ngo/bcic-care describes the EC\u0027s search logic. I\u0027ll update the doc to describe the ectool\u0027s logic.\n \n\u003e Here you\u0027re proposing to match the more specific config, but the doc says we match the first one.\n\nIIUC, ectool has access to the whole config list (\u003d json file) and it can examine every config because there is no strict (\u003dmilli-seconds) time constraint. Its purpose is to pick one right (\u003dmost specfic) config from the config library and write it to the CBI. (What\u0027s your thought from the factory perspective?)\n\nOn the other hand, CBI will store only one or a few configs. The EC needs to find a config quickly and the CBI space is limited. So, the logic is optimized accordingly.\n\nThe EC\u0027s search and ectool\u0027s search don\u0027t exactly behave the same and practically they never run in the same context (JSON, CBI, FW) so we should expect the search results to be different. That is, we can\u0027t assume the EC would find the same config as ectool even if they use the exactly the same logic. We need to prepare tests, tools, and processes for each (ec and ectool) individually.\n\n\u003e The additional suffix only happends after a space. (See more example here)\n\u003e How about we do prefix matching until we see a space?\n\nIt doesn\u0027t seem like a good idea to create a rule based on one vendor\u0027s (current) naming scheme. Also, the examples show there is a constant part even **after** a space char.",
      "parentUuid": "7dd99dd5_8ac5137a",
